# Steve Jobs

Steven Paul Jobs (1955–2011) was an American business magnate, inventor, and investor who served as the co-founder, chairman, and chief executive officer (CEO) of Apple. He is widely recognized as a pioneering figure of the personal computer revolution of the 1970s and 1980s, alongside his early business partner and fellow Apple co-founder Steve Wozniak. After achieving massive early success with the Apple II and introducing the graphical user interface to the mass market with the Macintosh, Jobs was forced out of Apple in 1985 following a lengthy power struggle with the company's board of directors and then-CEO John Sculley. He subsequently founded NeXT, a computer platform development company specializing in the higher-education and business markets, and funded the computer graphics division of Lucasfilm, which later became the acclaimed animation studio Pixar. In 1997, Apple acquired NeXT, allowing Jobs to return as CEO and rescue the company from the verge of bankruptcy. Under his renewed leadership, Apple introduced a series of highly successful, paradigm-shifting consumer electronics and software platforms, most notably the iMac, iPod, iPhone, and iPad, as well as the iTunes Store and the App Store. Jobs's design philosophy prioritized aesthetic minimalism, vertically integrated hardware-software ecosystems, and intuitive user interfaces, profoundly shaping modern consumer technology. He died in 2011 from complications related to a rare form of pancreatic cancer.

## Early Life and Education

Steven Paul Jobs was born on February 24, 1955, in San Francisco, California, to Joanne Schieble and Abdulfattah Jandali. He was adopted shortly after birth by Paul and Clara Jobs, who relocated the family to Mountain View, California, in the heart of what would soon become known as Silicon Valley. Growing up in a region heavily populated by engineering firms, Jobs developed an early interest in electronics, frequently tinkering with components in his family's garage.

Jobs attended Homestead High School in Cupertino, where he was introduced to Steve Wozniak, a technically gifted student who shared his fascination with electronics and Bob Dylan. In 1972, Jobs enrolled at Reed College in Portland, Oregon, but formally dropped out after one semester due to the financial burden on his parents. He remained on campus for another 18 months, sleeping on friends' floors and auditing classes that interested him, most notably a calligraphy course that he later credited with inspiring the diverse typography of the Macintosh. In 1974, he traveled to India seeking spiritual enlightenment, studying Zen Buddhism before returning to the United States to work as a technician for the video game manufacturer Atari.

## Apple Computer and the Macintosh

In 1976, Wozniak single-handedly designed the Apple I, a bare-bones motherboard. Recognizing its commercial potential, Jobs convinced Wozniak to sell it, leading to the formation of Apple Computer Company (now Apple Inc.) in the Jobs family garage.

The company's breakthrough came with the Apple II in 1977. Designed primarily by Wozniak but aggressively marketed and packaged by Jobs, the Apple II was one of the first highly successful mass-produced microcomputers, establishing the personal computing industry and transforming Apple into a lucrative corporation.

In 1979, Jobs toured the Xerox PARC research facility, where he saw the Alto, an early computer featuring a mouse-driven graphical user interface (GUI). Convinced this was the future of computing, Jobs integrated the GUI concept into Apple's next generation of computers. Following the commercial failure of the expensive Apple Lisa, Jobs focused on the Macintosh, which launched in 1984 with a famously cinematic Super Bowl commercial. Despite early enthusiasm, the Macintosh struggled with high pricing and limited software. Growing tensions over the company's direction led to a boardroom showdown between Jobs and CEO John Sculley in 1985. Stripped of his operational duties, Jobs resigned from Apple in protest.

## NeXT and Pixar

Following his departure from Apple, Jobs invested $7 million to found NeXT Inc., aiming to build high-end workstations for the academic and scientific markets. While NeXT computers were technologically advanced—featuring the object-oriented NeXTSTEP operating system and an integrated optical drive—they proved too expensive for widespread commercial success. However, the software architecture of NeXTSTEP would later prove highly influential.

In 1986, Jobs purchased the computer graphics division of Lucasfilm for $10 million, establishing it as an independent company named Pixar. Initially focused on selling high-end computer graphics hardware, the company struggled financially until it pivoted entirely to producing computer-animated films under the creative direction of John Lasseter. In 1995, Pixar released *Toy Story*, the first fully computer-animated feature film, which became a critical and commercial blockbuster. Pixar's subsequent initial public offering (IPO) made Jobs a billionaire. In 2006, The Walt Disney Company acquired Pixar, making Jobs Disney's largest individual shareholder and securing him a seat on its board of directors.

## Return to Apple and Revival

By 1996, Apple was operating at a massive financial loss and desperately needed a modern operating system to replace the aging Mac OS. The company purchased NeXT for $400 million, bringing Jobs back as an advisor. By 1997, the board ousted then-CEO Gil Amelio, and Jobs was appointed interim CEO.

Jobs quickly implemented a ruthless restructuring, cutting 70% of Apple's product lines to focus on a simplified grid of desktop and portable computers for consumers and professionals. He also forged a peace-making alliance with Microsoft, securing a vital cash injection and a commitment to continue producing Microsoft Office for the Mac. In 1998, Apple introduced the iMac G3, a colorful, all-in-one computer that eliminated legacy ports like the floppy drive in favor of USB. The iMac was a massive sales success and restored Apple to profitability. Jobs subsequently led the development of Mac OS X, an entirely new operating system built directly upon the Unix-based foundation of NeXTSTEP.

## The Mobile Revolution

Under Jobs's direction in the 21st century, Apple expanded beyond personal computers and became a dominant force in consumer electronics and digital media distribution.

### iPod and iTunes

In 2001, Apple released the iPod, a portable digital music player that combined a minimalist interface with a small hard drive capable of holding "1,000 songs in your pocket." To support the hardware, Jobs negotiated aggressively with major record labels to launch the iTunes Store in 2003, creating a legal, seamless ecosystem for purchasing digital music that revolutionized the recording industry.

### iPhone and iPad

On January 9, 2007, Jobs unveiled the iPhone, a device that merged an iPod, a mobile phone, and an internet communicator into a single product featuring a revolutionary multi-touch interface. The iPhone fundamentally disrupted the telecommunications industry, shifting the smartphone paradigm away from physical keyboards toward expansive glass touchscreens and app-based ecosystems. The introduction of the App Store in 2008 further cemented Apple's dominance by creating a highly lucrative software distribution platform.

In 2010, Jobs introduced the iPad, effectively creating the modern tablet computing market. The rapid succession of these wildly successful products propelled Apple to become the most valuable publicly traded company in the world by the summer of 2011.

## Illness and Death

In October 2003, Jobs was diagnosed with a neuroendocrine tumor, a rare but treatable form of pancreatic cancer. Initially refusing conventional medical intervention, he opted for alternative medicine diets before eventually undergoing surgery in 2004. His health visibly declined over the following years, leading to a liver transplant in 2009 and multiple medical leaves of absence.

On August 24, 2011, stating he could no longer meet his duties and expectations, Jobs resigned as CEO of Apple, passing the leadership to his chief operating officer, Tim Cook. Jobs assumed the role of chairman of the board but died just over a month later on October 5, 2011, at his home in Palo Alto, California, at the age of 56.

## Notable Creations and Product Introductions

While Jobs was not a programmer or a solo hardware engineer, he is credited as the chief architect, visionary, and co-inventor on hundreds of Apple patents and products, including:

* Apple II (1977)
* Macintosh (1984)
* NeXT Computer (1988)
* iMac (1998)
* iPod (2001)
* iPhone (2007)
* iPad (2010)

